From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 84/84] local: remoteproc: qcom_q6v5: fall back to the SMP2P
 line level when waiting for start

With 0082 and 0083 in place a cross-boot hibernate resume brings the CDSP back
but not the ADSP, and the difference between the two is an accident of timing
that points straight at the remaining bug:

  qcom_smp2p smp2p-adsp: hibernate: resynced smem,
      in 235b9885->235b9885 out 6982e7ab->6982e7ab, 2 inbound entries
  qcom_smp2p smp2p-cdsp: hibernate: resynced smem,
      in 9f2681d5->0000000000000000 out 3e48e268->3e48e268, 0 inbound entries

  qcom_q6v5_pas 32300000.remoteproc: hibernate: booted after restore
  qcom_q6v5_pas 6800000.remoteproc: hibernate: boot after restore failed: -110

Note what the pointers say: nothing moved. The SMEM items are exactly where
they were, so the "stale pointer" theory 0083 was written on is wrong as a
description of the ADSP. What actually differs is entry->last_value.

For the CDSP the remote had not published its item yet, so qcom_smem_get()
failed, smp2p->in stayed NULL and last_value stayed 0. The first doorbell then
went through the normal qcom_smp2p_intr() acquire path and computed
status = val ^ 0 = val, which is a change, so the ready sub-interrupt fired.

For the ADSP the item was already there -- the bootloader started that DSP --
so qcom_smp2p_start_in() latched last_value = readl(entry->value), capturing
the *previous* incarnation's asserted ready bit. When the instance we then
authenticated asserted ready again, status was 0, no edge was raised, and
qcom_q6v5_wait_for_start() timed out on a remote that was already running.

Latching the current value is the right thing for smp2p to do -- zeroing it
instead would re-deliver every set bit as a rising edge, including fatal and
stop -- so fix it where the assumption actually lives. On timeout, consult the
line level before declaring failure: smp2p_irq_get_irqchip_state() reads the
bit straight out of the shared item with readl(), so it is immune to whatever
last_value happens to hold. This is the same source qcom_q6v5_read_smp2p_state()
already uses to detect a remote that was running at probe.

The fatal and stop bits are required to be clear as well. Without that, a ready
bit left behind in SMEM by a remote that did *not* come back would read as a
successful start; with it, only a remote that is genuinely up and not in error
passes.

Costs one full timeout on the affected path, which is the price of the edge
having been missed. Nothing changes on a normal boot, where the edge arrives
and wait_for_completion_timeout() returns early.
---
 drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -183,10 +183,44 @@
  */
 int qcom_q6v5_wait_for_start(struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5, int timeout)
 {
+	bool ready = false;
+	bool fatal = false;
+	bool stop = false;
 	int ret;
 
 	ret = wait_for_completion_timeout(&q6v5->start_done, timeout);
-	return !ret ? -ETIMEDOUT : 0;
+	if (ret)
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * The start signal is an edge on an SMP2P bit, and SMP2P only raises
+	 * one when the bit differs from the value it cached the last time it
+	 * looked. After a cross-boot hibernate resume that cache describes the
+	 * *previous* incarnation of the remote: if that one had already
+	 * asserted ready -- and it had, the resuming kernel's bootloader
+	 * started it -- then the instance we just authenticated asserting it
+	 * again is not a change, no interrupt is delivered, and this wait
+	 * times out even though the remote is up.
+	 *
+	 * The line level does not have that problem; SMP2P reads it straight
+	 * out of the shared item. So before giving up, ask for it -- the same
+	 * source qcom_q6v5_read_smp2p_state() uses to spot a remote that was
+	 * already running at probe. Require the error bits to be clear as
+	 * well, so a stale ready left behind by a remote that did not come
+	 * back cannot be mistaken for a successful start.
+	 */
+	if (irq_get_irqchip_state(q6v5->ready_irq, IRQCHIP_STATE_LINE_LEVEL, &ready))
+		return -ETIMEDOUT;
+
+	irq_get_irqchip_state(q6v5->fatal_irq, IRQCHIP_STATE_LINE_LEVEL, &fatal);
+	irq_get_irqchip_state(q6v5->stop_irq, IRQCHIP_STATE_LINE_LEVEL, &stop);
+
+	if (!ready || fatal || stop)
+		return -ETIMEDOUT;
+
+	dev_info(q6v5->dev, "start signalled without an edge, took the line level\n");
+
+	return 0;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_q6v5_wait_for_start);
 
